      "text": "The roots of the equation $z^{1997}-1=0$ are the 1997th roots of unity, so we can write them as $e^{2\\pi i k/1997}$ for $k=0,1,2,\\ldots,1996$.\n\nThe sum of any two of these roots, $v+w$, can be written as $e^{2\\pi i k/1997} + e^{2\\pi i m/1997} = 2 \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi k}{1997})\\right)\\cos\\left(2\\pi (k - m)/1997\\right)$.  Using the second identity above, the condition $\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}\\le\\left|v+w\\right|$ becomes $\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi k}{1997}\\right)\\le\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.  Setting $t = \\frac{2\\pi k}{1997}$ for the purpose of simplification, this reduces to $\\cos{t}\\le-\\cos({\\pi/12})$ for $k=0,\n\n    3, 17, 67, 1972, 5948$.  This occurs twice for each even $k$ and once for each odd $k$, for a total of $\\frac{1997-1}{2}\\cdot2 +  \\frac{1997+1}{2}\\cdot1 = 1998$ possibilities.\n\nThe probability that $v$ and $w$ satisfy this condition is equal to the number of favorable outcomes $\\boxed{999}$ (since it is one half the total number of pairs), over the total number of possible events (ie, 3996 root pairs), so it is therefore equal to $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}$ .\n\nMy solution is similar, except instead of thinking about the distance between the roots, I thought about the average of the roots.  Brute force works too.",
